// @test: typecheck-fail
// @level: L0
// @tier: 0
// @expected-error: E402
// @tags: modules, mount, name-resolution, diagnostics
//
// Pin (T1011) — mounting a module nobody knows must be refused, whether
// the path has one segment or several.
//
// Before this fix:
//
//     mount nosuch.module.at.all;    error<E402>: module not found
//     mount zzz;                     compiled. exit code zero.
//
// The diagnostic existed and simply did not cover the one-segment case.
// The mount arm read
//
//     if <module found in registry>            { import everything }
//     else if <the path contains a dot>        { treat as module.item }
//
// and there was no third branch, so a single-segment path that resolved
// to nothing fell out of the chain and was ACCEPTED. Nothing was bound.
// A typo in a one-segment mount produced a green compile, and the
// failure surfaced later at the use site — or never, because every
// public stdlib name is visible without a mount anyway.
//
// Two entries in the compiler's since-deleted list of `core/` directory
// names were reachable only through this hole: `async_`, which has no
// directory at all, and `target`, which is the gitignored Cargo build
// directory. Both were "accepted" mounts of modules that do not exist.
//
// Refusing here cannot shadow a legitimate module: by the time control
// reaches this branch, the inline modules, the registry and the archive
// metadata have all been asked and all said no. A project module of its
// own name is answered by the registry branch, which runs first.

mount zzz;

fn main() {
    print("ok");
}
